## TokenUsage rows actually booked

9 rows were written to the dev Postgres by this plan: 2 from the single-post run, 0 from the
backlog sweep, 5 from the resume run and 2 from the second attempt. Together with the one
pre-existing probe row from plan 03-08 that makes 6 `translate-post` rows and 4
`translate-verify` rows in the last 24 hours, which is what the `--post-run` spend line
reports.

The backlog sweep booked zero rows despite making 22 of the run's model calls. This is not a
concurrency artefact and it is worth carrying into 03-10: spend is booked once, at the end of
a run, and the post-run count assertion throws before it. Any run that trips that assertion,
or is interrupted, loses its entire Postgres spend record. The per-post usage survives in the
run-state artifact either way, so nothing is unknowable, but it is not on the books. A
26-post production run is the case where this matters.

## Gate blocks: diagnosis

Three gate blocks occurred, on two posts. None was worked around, and no draft was written for
a blocked post.

| Slug | Offset | Artifact |
|---|---|---|
| what-neuronomixer-stands-for-ai-data-and-the-future-of-systems | 8061 | gate-mismatch-what-neuronomixer-stands-for-ai-data-and-the-future-of-syste-2026-08-25T16-54-20.735Z.json |
| why-explainable-ai-matters-more-than-you-think | 3289 | gate-mismatch-why-explainable-ai-matters-more-than-you-think-2026-08-25T17-09-32.145Z.json |
| why-explainable-ai-matters-more-than-you-think | 3289 | gate-mismatch-why-explainable-ai-matters-more-than-you-think-2026-08-25T17-21-12.258Z.json |

Both fingerprints were diffed at the reported offset. The cause is identical in every case.

Source, at the offset (fingerprint form, translatable slots blanked to NUL):

    {"_key":"k114","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"k110","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\u0000"},{"_key":"k112","_type":"span","marks":["k111"],"text":"\u0000"},...

Translated, same offset:

    {"_key":"k114","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"k110","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":""},{"_key":"k112","_type":"span","marks":["k111"],"text":"\u0000"},...

The model returned an empty string for that slot. The source text of both offending slots was
fetched to confirm it:

- `why-explainable-ai-matters-more-than-you-think`, block k51, children k47/k49/k50:
  `["The ", "EU AI Act", ", which enters its enforcement phase in August 2026, ..."]`
- `what-neuronomixer-stands-for-ai-data-and-the-future-of-systems`, block k114, children k110/k112/k113:
  `["The ", "European Commission defines Industry 5.0", " through three pillars: ..."]`

In both cases the lost slot is the bare English definite article "The " immediately before an
inline link. Persian has no definite article, so an empty response is a defensible translation
of that fragment in isolation. It is still content loss at the document level, and it is
invisible to the count check: the model returned exactly the demanded number of strings, one of
which was empty.

Why the gate catches it at all: `walkSlots` skips a span whose `text` is the empty string, so an
emptied slot stops being a translatable slot. The source fingerprint has that slot blanked to
NUL; the translated fingerprint has it as a literal empty string, because the walker no longer
enumerates it and so never blanks it. The two serialisations differ, and the D-05 tier 1 gate
refuses the write. That is the gate working, and it is the first time in this phase that it has
blocked real model output rather than a deliberately corrupted body.

The defect is per-call, not per-post. `why-explainable-ai-matters-more-than-you-think` blocked
on two separate attempts and passed on a third; `what-neuronomixer-stands-for-...` blocked once
and passed on its next attempt. Nothing about the post is unusual beyond having a bare article
before an inline link, which is ordinary English prose and is common in the production corpus.

Nothing was changed in `scripts/translate-posts.ts` to accommodate this. The plan says a blocked
post is the gate doing its job and must be diagnosed rather than worked around, and the model
transport is not this plan's to modify. The recommendation for 03-10 is recorded below.

## First-attempt response quality

Measured across 15 per-post translate attempts in this rehearsal:

- 6 first responses arrived wrapped in a markdown code fence (40.0 percent), despite the
  instruction hardening 03-08 added. The fence rate did not fall.
- 1 further first response was truncated or malformed JSON (`Expected ',' or ']' after array
  element at position 2178`).
- 7 of 15 first attempts therefore failed to parse (46.7 percent), each costing one retry.
- 1 post (`learning-excel-for-free-in-2025`, during the sweep) came back fenced on BOTH attempts
  and was reported as failed with no draft written. It was translated successfully on the next
  run. That is 1 post in 15 lost per pass to the fence alone.
- Total translate calls were 22 for 15 post attempts, so the fence and the malformed response
  together cost 47 percent more model calls than a clean run would have.

No fence stripping was added. A fenced response remains a broken contract.

## Overlapping runs

The backlog sweep was launched as a background command. The harness reported it as stopped after
roughly 66 minutes, and the resume run was started on that basis. The sweep had not stopped: it
kept running for a further 12 minutes and both runs were writing to `blog_posts_dev` at the same
time. This was an operator error in executing the plan and it is recorded rather than tidied away.

What it cost: the sweep's own post-run count assertion tripped, because the dataset had gained a
document the sweep did not create, and the sweep exited non-zero before booking its spend rows.

What it did not cost: nothing was corrupted and nothing was duplicated. Each post is created with
`createIfNotExists` under a fresh id, and the two runs never had the same post in a writable state
at the same time, because the two posts still outstanding when the resume started were the ones the
sweep had already gate-blocked. Verified afterwards, not assumed: the `--post-run` check asserts
that no two Farsi documents share a `translationOf` target and that every one resolves to an
existing source, and it passes over all eleven.

The assertion behaved correctly. It is a re-query rather than a success line precisely so that a
count it did not predict stops the run, and that is what happened.

## Carried into plan 03-10

1. An empty translated string should be rejected in code, before reassembly. `readStrings`
   already checks the count; it should also reject an empty string in a slot whose source string
   was non-empty. That turns a cryptic "fingerprint differs at offset 8061" into a named error,
   and it makes the condition retryable through the existing single retry instead of costing the
   post. The translate instruction should also say that no slot may come back empty. On this
   rehearsal's numbers, roughly one production post in six would otherwise block on it.
2. Spend booking is end-of-run only. A run that trips the count assertion or is interrupted
   loses every Postgres row for that run. Consider booking per post, or booking what has
   accumulated in a failure path.
3. The fence rate did not improve. 40 percent of first attempts, and one post lost outright to
   a double fence. 26 production posts at these rates means roughly 12 wasted calls and one or two
   posts needing a second run.
4. Never run two writing commands against one dataset at the same time, and do not trust a
   harness "stopped" notification for a long-running background write; confirm the process is gone.
